1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
By May 2025, the chief prosecutor

2
00:00:02,730 --> 00:00:06,800
of the International Criminal Court
had lost access to his email.

3
00:00:07,456 --> 00:00:08,896
Not because of a hack.

4
00:00:08,976 --> 00:00:11,116
Not because of a power cut.

5
00:00:11,336 --> 00:00:13,556
Because of a signature in Washington.

6
00:00:13,997 --> 00:00:17,297
That February, the United States
had put him under sanctions.

7
00:00:18,257 --> 00:00:21,697
And his email ran on Microsoft,
an American company.

8
00:00:22,309 --> 00:00:25,549
Microsoft says it never stopped
serving the court itself.

9
00:00:25,629 --> 00:00:27,989
But it was in contact with the court

10
00:00:28,219 --> 00:00:31,369
throughout the process
that led to his disconnection.

11
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:34,460
The exact chain of decisions is disputed.

12
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:36,960
But the dependency is not.

13
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:41,200
A sanction signed in Washington
reached an inbox in The Hague.

14
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:45,070
Now scale that up.

15
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:48,010
Your tax office,
your hospitals, your police

16
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:52,130
all depend on software and services
from a handful of companies abroad.

17
00:00:52,911 --> 00:00:57,171
Could your country still function if
that software doubled in price overnight?

18
00:00:57,991 --> 00:01:01,231
Or if its maker
was no longer allowed to sell it to you?

19
00:01:01,713 --> 00:01:03,193
Governments know all this.

20
00:01:03,273 --> 00:01:05,893
And they keep signing
the contracts anyway.

21
00:01:05,973 --> 00:01:07,173
So why?

22
00:01:09,171 --> 00:01:10,151
Take the Czech Republic.

23
00:01:10,381 --> 00:01:15,031
In September 2024,
its Interior Ministry signed

24
00:01:15,261 --> 00:01:17,791
a framework agreement
for Microsoft products.

25
00:01:18,527 --> 00:01:19,207
The ceiling?

26
00:01:19,287 --> 00:01:22,807
Just over fourteen billion Czech crowns,
before VAT.

27
00:01:22,987 --> 00:01:25,487
That's well over half a billion euros.

28
00:01:26,305 --> 00:01:30,285
It runs for four years,
covering Microsoft licences and support

29
00:01:30,515 --> 00:01:32,985
for ministries and other
state institutions.

30
00:01:33,761 --> 00:01:35,521
It's a ceiling, not a bill.

31
00:01:36,121 --> 00:01:37,721
The money is spent order by order,

32
00:01:37,951 --> 00:01:40,621
in smaller tenders between
approved suppliers.

33
00:01:41,561 --> 00:01:45,041
Pooling demand gives the government
more bargaining power.

34
00:01:45,801 --> 00:01:50,121
But it also makes the same ecosystem
the default across many institutions.

35
00:01:50,781 --> 00:01:52,581
And the Czechs are not unusual.

36
00:01:53,421 --> 00:01:57,301
A study for the European Parliament
found in December 2025

37
00:01:57,381 --> 00:02:01,941
that American firms dominate almost
every layer of Europe's digital stack.

38
00:02:04,511 --> 00:02:06,231
So how did it get like this?

39
00:02:06,871 --> 00:02:10,331
Mostly by making sensible decisions,
one at a time.

40
00:02:10,924 --> 00:02:14,404
A modern government
is a machine for processing information.

41
00:02:14,884 --> 00:02:20,204
Taxes, identity cards, court rulings,
land records, emergency calls.

42
00:02:20,578 --> 00:02:25,298
And inside its offices,
the software looks very familiar. Windows.

43
00:02:25,578 --> 00:02:29,678
Office. Outlook. Active Directory. Teams.

44
00:02:30,408 --> 00:02:31,728
Nothing exotic.

45
00:02:32,188 --> 00:02:34,828
The same tools millions of companies use.

46
00:02:34,908 --> 00:02:38,108
And that's exactly why
governments chose them.

47
00:02:38,615 --> 00:02:40,415
Staff already know them.

48
00:02:40,755 --> 00:02:43,075
Administrators know how to run them.

49
00:02:43,375 --> 00:02:46,235
Documents and applications expect them.

50
00:02:46,579 --> 00:02:48,699
When you need twenty thousand computers,

51
00:02:48,939 --> 00:02:51,339
picking what everyone knows
isn't a scandal.

52
00:02:52,239 --> 00:02:53,839
It's the obvious decision.

53
00:02:54,591 --> 00:02:56,951
But a company can change its strategy.

54
00:02:57,571 --> 00:03:00,911
A state can't simply stop working
while it thinks about it.

55
00:03:03,161 --> 00:03:04,721
So why not just switch?

56
00:03:05,341 --> 00:03:07,981
Picture a ministry
with twenty thousand staff.

57
00:03:08,678 --> 00:03:12,178
Accounts in Active Directory.
Email on Exchange.

58
00:03:12,678 --> 00:03:14,438
Workflows in SharePoint.

59
00:03:14,938 --> 00:03:17,478
Excel macros nobody remembers writing.

60
00:03:18,165 --> 00:03:22,045
Replacing that isn't a matter
of installing Linux and LibreOffice.

61
00:03:23,045 --> 00:03:25,865
You migrate documents,
rewrite applications,

62
00:03:26,065 --> 00:03:29,405
rebuild logins, retrain people,
and test everything.

63
00:03:29,681 --> 00:03:33,161
And you do it without the tax
office closing for a single day.

64
00:03:33,567 --> 00:03:35,627
That's called vendor lock-in.

65
00:03:35,907 --> 00:03:37,887
Leaving a supplier becomes so hard,

66
00:03:38,117 --> 00:03:41,447
or so expensive,
that it stops being a real option.

67
00:03:41,870 --> 00:03:43,730
And here's the strange part.

68
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:46,330
Nobody has to force you to stay.

69
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:49,490
There doesn't even need to be
a clause in the contract.

70
00:03:50,247 --> 00:03:54,087
Every year, more applications
are built on the system you already have.

71
00:03:54,727 --> 00:03:58,547
More staff learn it.
More data piles up in its formats.

72
00:03:59,414 --> 00:04:01,954
The technology itself
becomes the contract.

73
00:04:02,034 --> 00:04:05,274
And every year, the way out
gets a little longer.

74
00:04:07,424 --> 00:04:10,204
Now suppose the supplier
raises its prices.

75
00:04:10,804 --> 00:04:14,364
Or moves a feature you rely on
into a more expensive subscription.

76
00:04:15,051 --> 00:04:18,531
You can negotiate.
You can threaten to leave.

77
00:04:19,331 --> 00:04:23,991
But if leaving takes five years and costs
billions, everyone at the table knows it.

78
00:04:24,516 --> 00:04:27,996
The British government
has official guidance on exactly this.

79
00:04:28,716 --> 00:04:31,196
It warns that switching providers
can become difficult,

80
00:04:31,426 --> 00:04:34,896
time-consuming
and disproportionately expensive.

81
00:04:35,407 --> 00:04:37,407
And then it says something surprising.

82
00:04:38,347 --> 00:04:40,527
In the cloud, the advantages
of being locked in

83
00:04:40,757 --> 00:04:42,867
can often outweigh the disadvantages.

84
00:04:43,868 --> 00:04:46,808
So the question isn't
how to avoid every dependency.

85
00:04:47,768 --> 00:04:48,968
That's impossible.

86
00:04:49,568 --> 00:04:53,428
It's whether you know which ones you have,
and whether there's a real way out.

87
00:04:55,348 --> 00:04:57,508
And price is the smaller problem.

88
00:04:58,068 --> 00:05:00,308
The bigger one is what happened
in The Hague.

89
00:05:01,171 --> 00:05:03,971
A software company doesn't float
above the law.

90
00:05:04,771 --> 00:05:09,631
It answers to the country it belongs to,
including its sanctions and export rules.

91
00:05:10,379 --> 00:05:12,539
Most of the time, that never matters.

92
00:05:12,619 --> 00:05:16,419
Until relations between two countries
go bad.

93
00:05:17,026 --> 00:05:20,346
No, Microsoft can't switch off
the Czech Republic on a whim.

94
00:05:21,006 --> 00:05:24,366
There are contracts, courts,
and a lot of money in the way.

95
00:05:24,796 --> 00:05:27,976
But the court in The Hague
shows the real shape of the risk.

96
00:05:28,636 --> 00:05:30,636
Not a whole country going dark.

97
00:05:31,236 --> 00:05:33,796
One person,
targeted by another government.

98
00:05:36,026 --> 00:05:40,186
Think of the difference between buying
a computer and renting a service.

99
00:05:40,901 --> 00:05:44,701
A computer you bought keeps working
even if its maker disappears.

100
00:05:45,661 --> 00:05:49,881
A service you rent exists only
as long as someone keeps providing it.

101
00:05:50,364 --> 00:05:52,084
Which brings us to the data.

102
00:05:52,784 --> 00:05:56,944
A government might keep its files
in a data centre inside its own borders.

103
00:05:57,704 --> 00:05:59,304
That sounds reassuring.

104
00:05:59,843 --> 00:06:02,623
But where a server stands
doesn't decide which laws apply

105
00:06:02,853 --> 00:06:04,503
to the company running it.

106
00:06:04,995 --> 00:06:10,655
America's CLOUD Act of 2018
lets US authorities, with legal process,

107
00:06:10,835 --> 00:06:13,235
require American providers
to hand over data

108
00:06:13,465 --> 00:06:18,255
in their possession, custody or control,
even when it's stored abroad.

109
00:06:18,957 --> 00:06:23,697
In June 2025, the French Senate questioned
Microsoft France's director

110
00:06:23,927 --> 00:06:28,137
of public and legal affairs,
who was testifying under oath.

111
00:06:28,981 --> 00:06:31,721
Could he guarantee
that French citizens' data,

112
00:06:31,841 --> 00:06:34,181
entrusted to Microsoft by the state,

113
00:06:34,321 --> 00:06:39,001
would never be handed to US authorities
without France's explicit consent?

114
00:06:39,744 --> 00:06:41,324
No, he said, he couldn't.

115
00:06:41,404 --> 00:06:44,244
But he added that it had never happened.

116
00:06:45,024 --> 00:06:46,604
No such request, he said,

117
00:06:46,834 --> 00:06:50,304
had ever affected a European company
or public body.

118
00:06:50,873 --> 00:06:53,153
Both answers can be true at once.

119
00:06:53,833 --> 00:06:55,373
Where your data is stored and who has

120
00:06:55,603 --> 00:06:58,193
legal control over it
are two different things.

121
00:07:00,933 --> 00:07:05,033
At this point, someone is shouting
at the screen: just install Linux!

122
00:07:06,272 --> 00:07:11,772
Munich tried. Starting in 2004,
the city moved around fifteen thousand

123
00:07:11,852 --> 00:07:15,272
office computers
to its own Linux, called LiMux.

124
00:07:15,885 --> 00:07:19,525
In 2017, three years after
a new mayor took office,

125
00:07:19,745 --> 00:07:22,425
the city council voted
to go back to Windows.

126
00:07:23,191 --> 00:07:25,691
Why Munich reversed course
is still debated.

127
00:07:26,251 --> 00:07:29,531
But the desktop migration
was only one part.

128
00:07:30,191 --> 00:07:32,471
The harder challenge
was everything around it:

129
00:07:32,551 --> 00:07:36,211
old applications, support, users
and political commitment.

130
00:07:38,461 --> 00:07:40,261
Others are trying now.

131
00:07:40,821 --> 00:07:44,361
The German state of Schleswig-Holstein
has moved nearly eighty per cent

132
00:07:44,458 --> 00:07:48,221
of its workstations outside
the tax offices to LibreOffice.

133
00:07:49,503 --> 00:07:53,843
It has moved almost forty-four thousand
mailboxes to open-source email.

134
00:07:54,783 --> 00:07:58,923
And it says it already saves more than
fifteen million euros in licence fees.

135
00:07:59,671 --> 00:08:03,711
In its State Chancellery, more than two
hundred people now work on Linux.

136
00:08:04,581 --> 00:08:09,051
About twenty desks are still waiting,
tied to specialised applications.

137
00:08:09,851 --> 00:08:12,811
And the International Criminal Court,
the one in The Hague,

138
00:08:12,891 --> 00:08:17,161
confirmed in October 2025
that it would move to openDesk,

139
00:08:17,371 --> 00:08:20,871
an open-source office suite
developed for the German government.

140
00:08:23,131 --> 00:08:24,791
Open source has real advantages.

141
00:08:25,021 --> 00:08:29,271
Anyone can inspect the code.
You can change it.

142
00:08:29,501 --> 00:08:32,971
And more than one company can maintain it.

143
00:08:33,316 --> 00:08:35,876
But open source isn't free infrastructure.

144
00:08:36,756 --> 00:08:40,936
Someone still has to patch it, support it,
and fix it at three in the morning.

145
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:43,941
So the real goal
isn't to get rid of Microsoft.

146
00:08:44,841 --> 00:08:48,541
It's to make sure Microsoft
isn't the only realistic option.

147
00:08:51,361 --> 00:08:54,021
That's what people mean
by digital sovereignty.

148
00:08:54,861 --> 00:08:57,901
It sounds like a slogan,
but the idea is practical.

149
00:08:58,648 --> 00:09:02,628
No country can make every chip
and write every program itself.

150
00:09:02,708 --> 00:09:06,288
But it can know where
its critical dependencies are,

151
00:09:06,368 --> 00:09:10,168
keep control of its data,
and insist on open standards.

152
00:09:10,885 --> 00:09:13,705
The aim isn't to become
technologically isolated.

153
00:09:14,545 --> 00:09:17,485
It's to avoid becoming
technologically helpless.

154
00:09:20,075 --> 00:09:21,775
Here's a simple test.

155
00:09:22,535 --> 00:09:25,475
If your biggest software supplier
stopped serving you tomorrow,

156
00:09:25,555 --> 00:09:27,315
could you still reach your own data?

157
00:09:28,045 --> 00:09:29,625
Could you move your applications?

158
00:09:30,385 --> 00:09:31,365
Could someone else take over?

159
00:09:31,595 --> 00:09:35,725
And how long would it take?
A week. A month. Five years.

160
00:09:36,359 --> 00:09:38,759
If nobody knows, that's the answer.

161
00:09:39,499 --> 00:09:43,139
An exit plan that exists only on paper
isn't an exit plan.

162
00:09:43,817 --> 00:09:45,537
It has to be technically possible,

163
00:09:45,617 --> 00:09:47,537
affordable, and tested before the crisis,

164
00:09:47,767 --> 00:09:49,177
not during it.

165
00:09:51,707 --> 00:09:54,707
Buying software from a company
isn't the mistake.

166
00:09:55,507 --> 00:09:59,087
Companies often build it far better
than governments ever could.

167
00:09:59,698 --> 00:10:03,118
The mistake is becoming unable
to function without them.

168
00:10:04,808 --> 00:10:07,748
In the end, that's what
digital sovereignty means.

169
00:10:08,308 --> 00:10:10,548
Being able to choose
your technology suppliers.

170
00:10:10,628 --> 00:10:12,688
And being able to choose again.

171
00:10:13,911 --> 00:10:16,091
A government can rent its software.

172
00:10:16,671 --> 00:10:18,911
It shouldn't rent its ability to function.

173
00:10:22,101 --> 00:10:23,981
So here's a question for you.

174
00:10:24,661 --> 00:10:26,541
If your country's
biggest software supplier

175
00:10:26,771 --> 00:10:29,021
walked away tomorrow,
what would break first?

176
00:10:29,561 --> 00:10:30,991
Let me know in the comments.
